<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Biobío River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Biobío is one of the longest rivers of Chile, rising in the Andes and flowing north-west to the Pacific at Concepción. For three centuries after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> victory at Curalaba in 1598 it marked the frontier between the Spanish colony and the independent Mapuche lands to the south. Chile occupied the territory beyond it only in the 1860s–1880s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/biobio-river.jpg" alt="Biobío River" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcelo Ríos Marchant · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rios_Laja_y_Biob%C3%ADo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
